in this video i'm going to share with  you my five solar passive design tips  what's going on guys my name is david  thomas i'm a registered architect here  in western australia and today we're  starting the discussion with orientation  of your main living spaces first of all  if you're living in the northern  hemisphere compared to the southern  hemisphere they're going to be  completely opposite so throughout this  video i'm going to be using the southern  hemisphere you want to make sure that  all of your living spaces your kitchen  dining your kitchen is based in the  northern side of the block so it can get  the maximum solar orientation from that  sun throughout the day you want to then  bring your sleeping quarters to the rear  of the property being the south or the  north if you're in the northern  hemisphere traditionally speaking people  are more comfortable sleeping in the  cooler environment of the home hence why  you push the sleeping quarters to the  back or the south of the property next  you want to focus on thermal mass and  you want to be very strategic where you  locate your thermal mass in the property  as an example your concrete slab could  be considered your main area of thermal  mass or it could be something like a  rammed earth wall anything that  basically has a high quality of mass  it's quite obvious what has mass and  what doesn't have mass if it's heavy it  has mass if it's light it doesn't if you  only have one primary thermal mass wall  in your home that's going to radiate  heat throughout that entire space then  you want to locate that as close to your  living area windows as possible because  that sunlight is going to penetrate  through those windows hit that thermal  mass wall absorb all that heat and then  radiate it back out throughout the day  if for example you live in an extremely  cold climate that doesn't get much sun  at all you can locate that thermal mass  wall or thermal mass area on the floor  next to a heating source so if you have  a good fire log heater gas heater  whatever kind of heating source you want  to locate that thermal mass wall as  close to it as possible to be able to  then basically absorb that heat and  distribute it when that fire is no  longer active now i know everybody loves  their windows everybody loves looking at  the view however you really want to be  able to minimize your glazing throughout  the house to make sure that you're not  releasing too much of the actual heat so  you want to focus on the northern wall  again for the southern hemisphere and  aim to have about 15 to 25 of the floor  area as actual glazing you can go over  that percentage obviously that is just a  guide and you want to be using them  specifically for your project however if  you do go over that percentage it tends  to traditionally make the build cost a  little bit more expensive as that number  progressively gets higher over 25 going  back one step and if you're living in  one of those climate zones that is  extremely cold and you cannot heat or  cool your house effectively throughout  the year you might want to consider an  airlock so traditionally speaking an  airlock is going to be a small little  confined space completely sealed off so  that once you walk in the cold air or  the hot air from the outside doesn't  immediately distribute throughout your  entire space last but not least this is  critical to any house you live in it can  be something that you action today it  can be something that you plan for  tomorrow or it can be something that you  simply remember for the rest of your  life the main point here is that air  leaks will destroy any good solar  passive design and that's all from me  thanks so much for watching i'll see you  next time
 

